<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  TEMPLATE — build.ps1 / build.sh render this into network_security_config.xml
  and drop this file's own .template suffix. Do not edit the generated file;
  edit this one, or pass -CleartextHosts to the build.

  WHY CLEARTEXT AT ALL
  DSH is served over plain HTTP. The transport that actually protects it is
  WireGuard (Tailscale), which encrypts every packet between the phone and the
  desktop. Adding TLS on top would mean a self-signed certificate the WebView
  refuses, or a `tailscale cert` name — neither is required for the tunnel to be
  private. So cleartext is permitted, and the network layer carries the
  confidentiality.

  THE REAL BOUNDARY IS NOT THIS FILE
  It is the desktop firewall rule that pins the DSH port to the Tailscale
  interface (see README, "Step 1"). This file cannot enforce that, and neither
  can the app. If you bind DSH to 0.0.0.0 without that rule, you have put a
  shell on your LAN no matter what this configuration says.

  WHY cleartextTrafficPermitted IS NOT SCOPED BY DEFAULT
  Android's network security config matches literal hosts only — it has no
  CIDR or wildcard support, so "allow 100.64.0.0/10 and RFC1918" is not
  expressible. Since a tailnet address is whatever your tailnet assigned, the
  default below permits cleartext to any host and leans on the firewall rule.

  To narrow it, pass your own address at build time:

    .\build.ps1 -CleartextHosts 100.x.y.z
    .\build.ps1 -CleartextHosts 100.x.y.z,dsh-desktop.tailXXXX.ts.net
    ./build.sh with the cleartext-hosts option

  That renders a deny-by-default base-config plus a <domain-config> allowlist.
  Note the tradeoff: the generated allowlist is fixed at build time, so the app
  will refuse cleartext to any host you did not list. Only do this once your
  desktop address is stable.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="@@CLEARTEXT_BASE@@">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
@@DOMAIN_CONFIGS@@
</network-security-config>
